WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.356
Cookies carry important security behavior through attributes such as
Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, domain, path, and expiration.

2
00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:11.663
Authentication cookies and session cookies should be scoped deliberately.

3
00:00:11.663 --> 00:00:15.905
Secure aligns cookie transmission with secure transport where supported.

4
00:00:15.905 --> 00:00:19.814
HttpOnly helps reduce unnecessary access from client-side scripts.

5
00:00:19.814 --> 00:00:22.730
SameSite influences cross-site request behavior.

6
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.640
Domain and path scope determine where cookies are eligible to be sent.

7
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.893
Expiration affects session lifecycle.

8
00:00:28.893 --> 00:00:32.339
Cookie safety needs to align with the rest of the application.

9
00:00:32.339 --> 00:00:35.321
A Secure cookie assumes HTTPS is working as intended.

10
00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:39.629
A SameSite setting should match the browser flows the application
supports.

11
00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:43.539
A broad cookie domain can expose cookies to more hosts than necessary.

12
00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:49.437
A long-lived session cookie can increase exposure when devices are shared
or sessions are not monitored.

13
00:00:49.437 --> 00:00:53.612
Headers express policy, but the session design gives that policy meaning.

14
00:00:53.612 --> 00:00:57.124
Cache-control matters for sensitive pages and API responses.

15
00:00:57.124 --> 00:01:03.883
Browsers, shared caches, proxy caches, and intermediate systems may store
responses depending on headers and context.

16
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:12.763
Account pages, administrative pages, personalized API responses, files,
exports, and error responses may need stricter cache behavior than public
assets.

17
00:01:12.763 --> 00:01:19.589
File downloads may also need careful content disposition and content type
handling so the client treats them as intended.

18
00:01:19.589 --> 00:01:23.897
Sensitive data should not be placed casually in headers, URLs, or
referrers.

19
00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:30.590
Tokens and secrets in URLs can appear in logs, browser history, analytics,
referrer headers, and support screenshots.

20
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.964
Session safety, cache safety, and header safety should be designed
together.

21
00:01:34.964 --> 00:01:44.440
The question is not only whether a response works, but where its sensitive
values might be stored, forwarded, cached, or observed after it leaves the
Python application.
